WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.330
Filmmakers tasked with adapting comic book
icons for the big-screen have their work cut

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:06.330
out for them.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:10.780
After all, redesigning a universe filled with
flashy super suits and living planets can

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.120
be a bit of a challenge.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.680
Plus, screwing up the balance of "faithful"
and "creative" means inspiring the fiery wrath

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.450
of comic book fanboys — and nobody wants
that.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:24.730
Luckily, the Afro-futuristic nation of Wakanda
and its denizens have been given one badass

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.560
makeover for the big-screen.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.420
And even though we're super into the aesthetic
that director Ryan Coogler's bringing to the

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.989
table, let's take a look at what the cast
of Black Panther should really look like.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:36.809
T'Challa/Black Panther

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:41.609
The man behind the Black Panther mask, T'Challa
has been the king of Wakanda since his father's

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:46.100
death during the signing of the Sokovia Accords
in Captain America: Civil War.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.629
Like his Avengers ally Tony Stark, T'Challa
isn't afraid to switch up his gear every once

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:51.629
in a while.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:56.480
Since going toe-to-toe with Bucky Barnes,
also known as Winter Soldier, in his MCU debut,

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.370
T'Challa's snagged a spiffy new costume — a
lighter, energy-absorbent vibranium suit.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.370
This one boasts a more "tribal" aesthetic
reminiscent of illustrator Andy Park's early

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.770
concept design of the butt-kicking king's
Panther Mantle.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.140
Black Panther's new suit remains relatively
faithful to recent comic runs, and T'Challa

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.440
himself looks pretty close to his comic book
counterpart too.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.890
Where Chadwick Boseman's interpretation of
the character isn't afraid to rock the scruff,

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.610
however, his comic book equivalent is all
about keeping that chiseled jaw fur-free.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:30.200
Still, Boseman's T'Challa is a near-perfect
real-life interpretation of the king cat in

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.490
black.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:32.860
Ulysses Klaue

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.390
While Black Panther's titular hero is more
or less identical to his comic book double,

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.560
its main antagonist, Ulysses Klaue, has undergone
quite an overhaul.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.210
Portrayed by master of motion capture Andy
Serkis, Klaue has swapped out his comic counterpart's

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.550
bulky sonic blaster for a souped-up, retractable
prosthetic.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:54.160
But by far the biggest difference between
the charismatic arms dealer and his sinister

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.130
supervillain namesake is the fact that Serkis'
Klaue is obviously not a being made entirely

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.300
of living sound.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.090
Technically, Marvel Comics' Klaw was also
once a bearded human jerk.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.709
But his eventual redesign as a red-n'-purple
baddy and frequent nemesis of the Fantastic

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:13.599
Four is the far more memorable incarnation
of the one-armed heel.

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.040
Shuri

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.799
Sister of T'Challa and smartypants Vibranium
tinkerer, Princess Shuri is a force to be

00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:20.830
reckoned with.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.310
Not only is she the greatest scientific genius
this side of Stark Tower, but, in the comics,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.290
she's taken up the Panther Mantle on a number
of occasions in her brother's absence.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:33.420
In other words, like T'Challa, she's consumed
the Heart-Shaped Herb and reaped the same

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.909
superhuman powers.

00:02:34.909 --> 00:02:40.370
Shuri, portrayed by Letitia Wright in MCU's
Black Panther, has undergone a dramatic redesign.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.400
Chief among the differences is her outfit,
a sleeveless tunic and a full mantle with

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.659
neck coverage along with what appears to be
a chin guard.

00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:51.999
While comic book Shuri doesn't wear much makeup
save for the occasional strip of paint over

00:02:51.999 --> 00:02:56.920
her chin, the MCU's Wakandan princess adorns
herself with a series of white dots across

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.389
her forehead, brow, cheeks, and chin.

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:03.650
She also wears her hair in a braided updo
while her comic book counterpart typically

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.389
wears her hair short under an ornate headdress
or her Black Panther mask.

00:03:08.389 --> 00:03:09.389
Killmonger

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:14.840
In the comics, the exiled and shamed warrior
N'Jadaka adopts the pseudonym Killmonger,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.799
blaming both T'Challa's bloodline and Klaw
for his parents' demise.

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:23.170
In an intriguing twist, Michael B. Jordan's
interpretation of the character — a former

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:27.930
American black ops soldier named Erik Stevens
— teams up with the South African supervillain

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:28.930
Klaw.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.189
Instead of, y'know, beating him to a bloody
pulp the first chance he gets.

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:36.419
The MCU sees Killmonger — who earned his
nickname on the battlefield — transformed

00:03:36.419 --> 00:03:40.129
from a Herculean behemoth to a buff badass
with a sense of style.

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:44.700
The MCU's Erik Stevens may have lost his source
material's straight-up murder-y aura — but

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.920
he more than makes up for it with hip sensibilities,
combat know-how, and his very own Gold Jaguar

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.189
suit.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:52.459
Nakia

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:56.900
If Lupita Nyong'o has proven anything since
her award-winning breakout role in 2013's

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.980
12 Years a Slave, it's that she can do no
wrong.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:04.390
So casting her among Black Panther's pantheon
of brilliant actors must've seemed like a

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:05.390
no-brainer.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:10.609
Nyong'o portrays Nakia, a secret agent summoned
back to Wakanda at the behest of T'Challa.

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:14.890
In the Black Panther comics, Nakia teams up
with Killmonger and eventually adopts the

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.879
villain persona Malice after her obsession
with the titular Wakandan king lands her in

00:04:18.879 --> 00:04:19.879
hot water.

00:04:19.879 --> 00:04:24.090
While her comics counterpart is known for
her bead-adorned hair and jewelry, Nyong'o's

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:29.020
take on the character wears her hair in tight
curls and dons a variety of getups — including

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.490
full Dora Milaje armor.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:32.490
M'Baku

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:37.080
Thankfully, the Black Panther comics' problematically
named Man-Ape will be sticking with his birth

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.699
name — M'Baku — for his film debut.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:43.740
Producer Nate Moore, however, admits that
the gorilla gods are important to M'Baku's

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.190
tribe, the Jabari.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:50.310
Thus, while he's ditching the name, the Wakandan
throne-seeker and potential unlikely ally

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.139
to T'Challa will retain a touch of white fur
along his arms and legs.

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:58.509
His revamped wardrobe is a nod to his source
material's gruesome costume — a white gorilla

00:04:58.509 --> 00:05:03.139
hide that he donned after hunting a mythical
ape, skinning it, devouring its flesh, and

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:04.979
bathing in its blood.

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:05.979
Yikes.

00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:06.979
Ramonda

00:05:06.979 --> 00:05:10.480
"My son, it is your time."

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.710
Matriarch of Wakanda, wife of the late T'Chaka,
and mother of T'Challa and Shuri, Ramonda

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:19.550
has suffered greatly at the hands of her husband
and son's political rivals.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:23.990
Yet despite years of family tragedy, she's
proven herself to be a vital asset to the

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.330
Wakandan high council.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:29.700
In the Black Panther comics, Ramonda has been
depicted with both black and white hair.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:34.060
The MCU's film, on the other hand, sees Angela
Bassett's take on the character at her most

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:39.729
regal, rocking ivory dreadlocks and a wardrobe
fit for the Queen Mother of Wakanda.

00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:41.050
Zuri

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:45.960
In the Black Panther comics, Zuri was a personal
attendant to T'Chaka before the elder ruler's

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:46.960
death.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.810
He would go on to serve the same role for
his son, the young King T'Challa.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:54.800
That is, until the warrior's untimely demise
at the hands of Morlun, an energy vampire,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.220
in a sequence of events that's way too complicated
to talk about here.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.050
Suffice to say, Zuri plays a slightly different
role in the MCU.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:07.200
A spiritual "Obi-Wan" type, the Wakandan shaman-like
figure is something of an advisor to T'Challa

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.610
and his kin.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.800
Appearance-wise, these two interpretations
look like entirely different characters.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.000
While the Marvel Comics iteration of Zuri
is a hulking behemoth complete with bursting

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.259
abs and a neck thicker than most men's waists,
Forest Whitaker … isn't.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:25.129
That's not to say the acting veteran doesn't
look rad in his own right: trading his comic

00:06:25.129 --> 00:06:30.020
doppelganger's black mane for a bald dome
and adding a splash of color to his drab robes,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:34.669
Zuri's big-screen interpretation calls to
mind a wise elder figure.

00:06:34.669 --> 00:06:36.300
Everett Ross

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.240
Let's face it: Martin Freeman doesn't exactly
stand out in a crowd — and neither does

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.539
Black Panther comics' Everett K. Ross.

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:46.629
He's without question the least interesting
and least-super-powered guy in the mix.

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:51.610
But personality-wise, the cold, no-nonsense
CIA operative we met in Captain America: Civil

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:57.150
War is a total 180 from the Chandler Bing-inspired
diplomat tasked with escorting T'Challa onto

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.020
American soil in the comics.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:03.780
In the Black Panther film, Ross finds himself
involved in a conflict between Ulysses Klaue

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.180
and T'Challa, causing him to team up with
the Wakandan King and become one of his most

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:09.480
trusted allies.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.849
While these two versions of Ross are polar
opposites in terms of attitude, personality,

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:18.190
behavior, and just about everything else,
the suits at MCU did a bang-up job nailing

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:20.800
his clean-cut style — or, at least they
did in Civil War.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:27.080
Sure, they may both be average-looking, middle-aged
white dudes, but the perpetually-suited, too-sarcastic-for-his-own-good

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:31.270
diplomat who once sold his soul for a pair
of pants wouldn't be caught dead in a t-shirt

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:32.280
and casual jacket.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.139
OK, that's a stretch, we know.

00:07:34.139 --> 00:07:36.069
But there's really not much to say about this
guy.

00:07:36.069 --> 00:07:38.840
Could he be any more nondescript?

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.520
Ayo

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:45.409
Like Everett Ross, we briefly met Ayo in Civil
War during a confrontation between T'Challa's

00:07:45.409 --> 00:07:49.639
feisty head of security and Natasha Romanoff,
a.k.a Black Widow.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:53.379
While we learned little to nothing about Ayo's
character from her short scene, her single

00:07:53.379 --> 00:07:54.379
line of dialogue ...

00:07:54.379 --> 00:07:55.379
"Move.

00:07:55.379 --> 00:07:56.620
Or you will be moved."

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:01.539
...shined a light on what we could come to
expect from the Dora Milaje agent.

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:03.690
Keeping Black Panther safe is her top priority.

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:08.240
Ayo's onscreen presence seems more or less
in line with her comic book counterpart — they've

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.909
both vowed to protect their king and loved
ones on many an excursion across Wakanda and

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:13.909
beyond.

00:08:13.909 --> 00:08:17.740
That being said, comic book Ayo has a bit
more pizazz than her film doppelgänger, who

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:21.699
keeps things simple with a shaved head and
traditional Dora Milaje armor.

00:08:21.699 --> 00:08:26.629
In the comics, Ayo rocks a sick sci-fi shaved
head/ponytail combo along with glyph-like

00:08:26.629 --> 00:08:29.780
ink down her forehead and across her cheeks.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:31.090
Okoye

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:36.260
Like Ayo, Okoye is a member of T'Challa's
personal bodyguard unit, the Dora Milaje.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:40.800
And while a number of the fearsome sisterhood
soldiers wear their ink proudly in the comics,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.870
MCU Okoye's shaved head and unique angular
tattoos has her standing out from the squad.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.700
Danai Gurira's look is as close as you can
get to the comics' portrayal of the royal

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:52.760
band of butt-kickers — depending on the
run, that is.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:57.350
Black Panther comic writer Ta-Nehisi Coates'
contemporary take on the troop sees them rocking

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:00.010
their infamous facial tats and hip haircuts.

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:04.250
But Christopher Priest was the brains behind
their comic book debut, and his interpretation

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.639
of the bodyguards was more in line with your
typical femme fatales, impractical tight dresses

00:09:08.639 --> 00:09:10.180
and all.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.339
W'Kabi

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:15.970
Daniel Kaluuya's W'Kabi shares his comic book
counterpart's primary responsibilities as

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.149
chief of Wakandan border tribe security.

00:09:18.149 --> 00:09:22.730
In the Black Panther comics, W'Kabi would
prove to be an invaluable ally to T'Challa,

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:27.480
even becoming his second in command before
dying alongside Zuri at the hands of Morlun

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.160
— again, that dude whose arc we're not even
getting into.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:34.910
Like Zuri, W'Kabi's MCU interpretation differs
from his source material's incarnation so

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:39.209
dramatically that the uninformed might mistake
him for a brand new character.

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:43.180
While the Black Panther comics depict W'Kabi
with a bundle of wild dreadlocks and little

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:47.200
clothing — so as to not restrict him while
he's bashing skulls — Kaluuya's character

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.641
of the same name sticks to a relatively reserved
look, appearing more like an angry puppy than

00:09:51.641 --> 00:09:54.980
the grizzled, bionic-armed warrior we've come
to love — and mourn.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:55.980
Thanks for watching!

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.810
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:00.950
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

